# Monorepo conventions for {{.ProjectName}}

{{.ProjectName}} is configured as a **monorepo** (`features.monorepo`
in `.aikata/aikata.yaml`). aikata supports this layout by accepting
**nested `AGENTS.md` files**, one per app, layered on top of the
repository-root rules.

## Layout

```
{{.ProjectName}}/
├── AGENTS.md              # repository-wide rules (invariants)
├── SPEC.md / ARCHITECTURE.md / GLOSSARY.md
├── apps/
│   ├── README.md          # how to add an app under apps/
│   └── _example/
│       └── AGENTS.md      # per-app rule template (copy to make new apps)
└── docs/
    └── monorepo.md        # this file
```

`apps/_example/` is a template, not a real app. Underscore-prefixed
directories are skipped by tooling that walks `apps/`; copy it to
`apps/<your-app>/` to start a new app under the monorepo.

## Layered rules

When an agent works inside `apps/<name>/`, both `AGENTS.md` files
apply:

1. `apps/<name>/AGENTS.md` carries app-specific rules (its stack, its
   test runner, its deployment target, its style overrides).
2. `AGENTS.md` at the repository root carries monorepo-wide rules
   (cross-app coding conventions, shared CI, release policy, the
   `aikata generate` contract).

The root rules are inherited; the per-app rules **add to** the root,
they do not replace it. When the two conflict, the per-app file wins
within its directory tree because it is the more specific scope.

## Why not one giant `AGENTS.md`?

Because the rules that govern your Flutter app's pubspec workflow do
not also govern your TypeScript SDK's `npm publish` flow. Splitting
them keeps each file scannable and lets the agent load only what
matters for the directory it is working in.

If your project is small enough that one `AGENTS.md` would be fine,
do not enable `--monorepo` — the flag is opt-in for a reason.

## `aikata generate` in a monorepo

`aikata generate` reads the **root** `AGENTS.md` and projects it into
the per-tool files (`CLAUDE.md`, `.cursor/rules/main.mdc`) at the
repository root. Per-app `AGENTS.md` files are user-managed; aikata
does not regenerate them. Most AI tools (Claude Code, Cursor) already
read nested `AGENTS.md` files natively, so no per-app projection is
required.
